# Metal-Organic Frameworks: A Rapidly Growing Class of Versatile Nanoporous Materials

## abstract
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) represent a new class of hybrid organic-inorganic supramolecular materials comprised of ordered networks formed from organic electron donor linkers and metal cations. They can exhibit extremely high surface areas, as well as tunable pore size and functionality, and can act as hosts for a variety of guest molecules. Since their discovery, MOFs have enjoyed extensive exploration, with applications ranging from gas storage to drug delivery to sensing. This review covers advances in the MOF field from the past three years, focusing on applications, including gas separation, catalysis, drug delivery, optical and electronic applications, and sensing. We also summarize recent work on methods for MOF synthesis and computational modeling.

Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs) represent a new class of hybrid organicinorganic supramolecular materials comprised of ordered networks formed from organic electron donor linkers and metal cations. They can exhibit extremely high surface areas, as well as tunable pore size and functionality, and can act as hosts for a variety of guest molecules. Since their discovery, MOFs have enjoyed extensive exploration, with applications ranging from gas storage to drug delivery to sensing. This review covers advances in the MOF field from the past three years, focusing on applications, including gas separation, catalysis, drug delivery, optical and electronic applications, and sensing. We also summarize recent work on methods for MOF synthesis and computational modeling.
## 1. Introduction
Fifteen years have passed since the term “metal-organic framework” (MOF) first appeared in the literature 1  and more than a decade since the influential paper by Li et al. reporting the structure of the MOF-5. 2  The nanoporosity and incredibly high surface area of this material $(>3000\ \mathrm{m}^{2}\ \mathrm{g}^{-1})$ initiated a new field of supramolecular chemistry, in which more than 500 articles reporting materials described as MOFs appeared in 2009. MOFs possess unique properties among the various classes of microporous and mesoporous materials, creating interest for an unprecedented range of applications. A small number of MOFs and related materials, such as zeolite imidazolate frameworks (ZIFs), are now available commercially, although only as specialty chemicals.
MOFs are crystalline materials with structures based on classical coordination bonds between metal cations such as $\mathrm{Zn^{+2}}$ and electron donors such as carboxylates or amines. The self assembly of these components, typically in solution, creates rigid pores that do not collapse upon removal of solvent or other “guest” molecules occupying the pores following synthesis. The presence of both inorganic and organic components enables both the pore size and chemical environment to be tailored to achieve specific properties. This sets MOFs apart from zeolites, which are also crystalline and microporous, but are fully inorganic and thus lack synthetic flexibility. The topology of MOFs is intimately related to both the coordination environment favored by the metal ion and the geometry of the organic “linker” groups, which together form so-called secondary building units that establish the network symmetry. 3  These characteristics further distinguish MOFs from other classes of porous materials, such as aerogels, which are amorphous, and ordered or partially ordered materials created from block copolymers.
The early excitement over MOFs was largely focused on the use of MOFs for storage of gases such as hydrogen and methane. Carbon dioxide sequestration is currently gaining momentum, and to a lesser extent, chemical separations. These topics are, in a sense, “classical” applications of MOFs as microporous materials: they make use of their high surface areas, which increase van der Waals interactions needed for uptake of weakly interacting gases, and exploit the tunability of MOF pore dimensions to control relative rates of adsorption and transport through the pores. However, MOFs have properties that extend their potential range of use far beyond traditional microporous materials. These include: luminosity resulting from conjugated organic linkers; structural flexibility in response to molecular adsorption or changing environmental conditions; charge transfer (ligand-to-metal or metal-to-ligand); high thermal stability relative to many organic polymers; electronic and conducting properties; and pH-sensitive stability. As a result, there are now demonstrations of MOFs for chemical detection (using both optical and mechanical transduction mechanisms), radiation detection, ion-conducting membranes, and drug delivery, as well as catalysis (functioning either as the catalyst itself or as templates for catalyst nanoparticles), and both gas- and liquidphase chemical separations.
In this article, we summarize developments concerning the use of MOFs for applications that include both traditional uses of microporous materials, such as separations and catalysis, as well as applications in which the unique properties of MOFs set them apart from other porous materials. Gas storage, in particular storage of hydrogen, is not covered here, because this topic was reviewed recently. 4  Synthetic methods are reviewed as well, focusing on efforts to accelerate synthesis and/or scale it to larger quantities needed for practical uses. Finally, we provide a brief summary of recent developments in computational modeling of MOFs, as this field is demonstrating substantial progress toward the goal of rational design of MOF materials.
## 2. MOF Synthesis
With the immense interest in MOFs a multitude of new frameworks are being reported each year, many of them with new linkers and topologies. It is beyond the scope of this review to catalogue these discoveries here. We do, however, wish to describe recent advances in methods of MOF synthesis, since improvements in this area are needed for both scale up to larger quantities and to reduce the synthesis time. Typical MOF syntheses involve heating a mixture of ligand and metal salt in solvent for $12{-}48\mathrm{~h~}$ . While this procedure can yield high quality crystals, it suffers from long reaction times and can be difficult to scale above ${\sim}1\ \mathrm{g}$ . Solvents, especially the oftemployed diethylformamide (DEF), can be costly and again cause difficulty in scale up. Mueller and coworkers at BASF were the first to investigate scale up procedures for MOFs and reported a synthesis of MOF-5 on a $50\ \mathrm{g}$ scale that required almost $6\mathrm{~L~}$ of DEF. 5  Thus, methods that decrease reaction times and the amount of solvent required are highly desirable for laboratory scale experiments, as well as for commercialization of MOF materials. We will review recently reported microwave, sonochemical, and mechanochemical syntheses, as well as methods for catenation control and postsynthetic modification. Methods for growing MOF thin films are also being developed and Fischer and coworkers recently reviewed this topic. 6  We will touch on this when relevant in  this and subsequent sections.
## 2.1. Microwave Synthesis
Microwave heating has been employed broadly as a means of accelerating the rate of chemical reactions. 7 8  This rate enhancement is thought to be due to the high temperatures that can be rapidly achieved in microwave-absorbing solvents. MOFs were first synthesized via microwave irradiation by Ni and Masel, who produced IRMOF-1 (MOF-5), $^{-2}$ , and $^{-3}$ with vastly decreased reaction times and microcrystalline products. 9  This technique was then expanded to several other MOFs. 10 11
In the last few years, researchers have sought to better understand the mechanism of this technique and broaden its application. The mechanism of rate enhancement was investigated for growth of CuBTC (HKUST-1) 12  is thought primarily to be due to an increase in the rate of nucleation, not the rate of crystal growth. Interestingly, the reverse is true for MIL-53 (Fe), although both the rate of nucleation and crystal growth are accelerated. 13  Varying temperature and reaction times allow for the phase control of Cu-1,3,5-benzentriacarboxylate (BTC) MOFs, including a synthesis of CuBTC with higher surface area than that produced by solvatothermal or electrochemical synthesis. 14  Optimization of MOF-5 microwave synthesis revealed that heating for $0.5\mathrm{~h~}$ produces a material on a gram scale with very similar properties to MOF-5 synthesized with conventional
