Microwave heating, with its microcrystalline products and fast reaction times, can also be used to synthesize MOF thin films. For example, ZIF-8 membranes have been grown on titania using microwave heating 19  and MOF-5 films were synthesized on graphite coated anodized alumina via microwave irradiation to produce a tightly packed film. Using these substrates, surface features can be produced by employing patterned graphite surfaces. 20  To make continuous MOF-5 membranes, these seeded films can be subjected to traditional solvothermal synthesis. 21 ZIF-7 continuous membranes (Figure 2) have been also been grown from seeded substrates. In this case, the seeds were grown solvothermally and applied via dip coating in a dispersed polymer solution, followed by microwave irradiation to grow a thin $(1{-}2\ \upmu\mathrm{m})$ film. Short secondary growth times achievable with microwave heating are thought to influence the deposition rate. 22
## 2.2. Sonochemical Synthesis
Another method that produces small MOF crystals with decreased reaction times is sonochemical synthesis, which has only recently begun to be explored. The rate acceleration in sonochemical irradiation stems from the formation and collapse of bubbles in solution, termed acoustic cavitation, which produces very high local temperatures (greater than 5000 K) and pressures, resulting in extremely fast heating and cooling rates. 23  MOF-5 was synthesized with sonochemical irradiation in 1-methyl-2-pyrrolidinone (NMP) to produce $5{-}25\upmu\mathrm{m}$ crystals in 30 minutes, with very similar properties to MOF-5 synthesized via microwave and conventional heating. 16  Comparisons were carried out for MOF-177 18  and a $Z\mathrm{n}{-}2,2^{\prime}$ -bipyridine- $^{.5,5^{\prime}}$ - dicarboxylate $\mathrm{MOF^{[17]}}$ with similar results, although in the case of MOF-177 the material sonochemically synthesized has superior properties to that formed with microwave irradiation. Synthesis of CuBTC has also been achieved with reduced reaction times by this method. 24  Crystal size control is observed for $\mathrm{Zn}_{3}(\mathrm{BTC})_{2}{\cdot}12\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ with crystals ranging from $50{-}900~\mathrm{nm}$ depending on reaction time. 25  With varying reaction times, different phases of [Zn(1,4-benzenedicarboxylate) $(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})]_{\mathrm{n}}$ can be produced, from nanobelts and nanosheets to microcrystals. 26 Although a relatively new technique, sonochemical synthesis helps alleviate the long reaction times and high temperatures of solvothermal synthesis, but its generality is yet to be fully established.
## 2.3. Mechanochemical Synthesis
While microwave and sonochemical methods decrease MOF synthesis times, they still rely on solvents, whereas mechanochemical synthesis is a solvent-free methodology. In this technique, neat mixtures of linker molecules and metal salts are ground together in a ball mill to produce the desired MOF. Mechanochemical synthesis of a Cu-isonicotinic acid MOF was first reported in 2006 by Pichon et al. 27  Recently, this group sought to expand the technique by conducting an array-based study of five linkers and twelve Cu, Fe, and Zn salts. Of the sixty possible combinations, forty gave crystalline product, including
HKUST-1. 28  In general, linkers with lower melting points and metals that are either hydrated or release solvent upon reaction (e.g. acetic acid) promoted reactivity. Friscic and coworkers studied a variant of mechanochemical synthesis called liquid-assisted grinding (LAG), in which a very small amount of solvent is added to the reaction mixture. By varying the solvent added to a mixture of fumaric acid and $\mathsf{Z n O}$ , they were able to obtain 1D, 2D, and 3D coordination polymers. Addition of bipy or bpe linkers produced pillared MOFs with DMF, MeOH, EtOH, or $\mathrm{i{\cdot}P r O H}$ acting as the solvent. 29  This group demonstrated additional versatility of the method by preparing pillared bdc-DABCO MOFs (Figure 3), finding that by adding catalytic amounts of metal salts they could produce several different structures. 30  Nitrate salts promoted a tetragonal structure, while sulfates induced a hexagonal structure. Based on NMR labeling studies, it was determined that the salts provide a templating effect. While mechanochemical synthesis shows promise, its application to MOFs is still in its formative stages, and requires further investigation, as with the other methods described here.
## 2.4. Catenation Control
In the earlier works by Yaghi et al. regarding the IRMOF series, both interpenetrated and non-interpenetrated variants are reported. 31  Controlling this effect, however, was accomplished primarily via concentration, with high dilution required to obtain noncatenated structures, resulting in low yields. As interpenetration can substantially change the properties of MOFs, methods for its control are desirable. Zaworotko and coworkers systematically studied the effects of reaction temperature and concentration for a pillared Cd-bdc-bipy MOF and found that at $0.1\mathrm{~M~}$ reactant concentration, low temperatures $(85-95~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ afford the noninterpenetrated MOF, while higher temperatures $(115{-}125\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ give the interpenetrated structure. At higher dilutions (0.0125 M), only the noninterpenetrated MOF was formed. Farha et al. were able to control the catenation of a series of pyridal pillared $\mathrm{Zn}{-}4,4^{\prime},4^{\prime\prime},4^{\prime\prime\prime}$ -benzene-1,2,4,5-tetrayltetrabenzoic acid MOFs by introducing sterically bulky bromine groups to the acid, thereby blocking interpenetration in the xy-plane. This effect holds for a variety of pyridal pillars. The noninterpenetrated structures generally have greater uptake of $\mathrm{CO}_{2},$ although it should be noted that the bromine chemical functionality makes this observation more difficult to interpret. Shekhah et al. report the synthesis of noninterpenetrated MOF-508a films, grown via liquid-phase epitaxy. They attribute suppression of catenation to a templating effect on the functionalized gold surface used to grow the films. In a rare example of MOF purification, Farha et al. report the separation of IRMOF-9 and its noninterpenetrated counterpart IRMOF-10 via density: IRMOF-10 floats in a 4:5:26 $\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{Cl}_{2}\mathrm{:CHCl}_{3}\mathrm{:CH}_{2}\mathrm{BrCl}$ solution while IRMOF-9 does not (Figure 4). 32
## 2.5. Postsynthetic Modification
Postsynthetic modification (PSM) allows for the introduction of diverse chemical functionality into preexisting frameworks, eliminating the need to develop new conditions for MOF synthesis. This topic was recently reviewed by Wang and Cohen, 33 so here we focus on some of the latest advances. Two new reactions were recently explored that expand the scope of PSM of MOFs. First, Jones and Bauer demonstrated diasteriomeric control using the electrophilic bromination of a stilbene-based MOF. The reaction yields only one diasteriomer, as opposed to two obtained from the stilbene dicarboxylate linker in solution, due to the inability of the linker to rotate when locked into the framework. Partial bromination also leads to increased surface area, an effect attributed to disruption of inter-lattice interactions in the interpenetrated structure. 34
The second new PSM method, “Click” chemistry, the CuI catalyzed Huisgen cycloaddition of azides and alkynes, was also demonstrated recently. Click chemistry is known as an extremely efficient, robust reaction with high yields. 35  Goto et al. 36  used a variant of IRMOF-16 in which azides are appended to the linkers. This MOF was then reacted with acetylenes having ester, hydroxyl, and alkane functionalities, yielding a high level of incorporation and very little framework degradation. Acetylenes with amine or carboxylic acid functionality
