## 3. Computational Modeling
There has been explosive progress in classical molecular modeling of MOF materials in recent years. Rather than exhaustively reviewing this work in this section, we discuss the development of molecular simulation as a screening tool for MOF performance related to gas adsorption and separation, an application in which modeling is proving particularly effective. Specifically, we review advances in the following areas: 1) force field development; 2) analyte screening; and 3) separations/permeability. Gas adsorption isotherms and diffusion coefficients can be obtained from grand canonical Monte Carlo (GCMC) and molecular dynamics (MD) simulation, respectively. These simulation methods are validated by direct comparison with experiment and can therefore be used as a predictive tool in the development of MOFs for specific applications.
Although framework flexibility is important to some aspects of MOF simulations, such as guest diffusion, the derivation of a consistent set of force field parameters applicable to a wide range of MOF materials is proving difficult. Specifically, a classical model that includes framework flexibility must successfully describe the effect of d-orbital bonding involved in metalmetal, 40  metal-linker, 41  and metal-adsorbate 42–44  interactions. Tafipolsky and Schmid 45  recently used a genetic algorithm approach to derive force field parameters that include framework flexibility. This approach suffers from several limitations, however. First, despite the claim of algorithm efficiency, the application of this approach to new framework materials requires costly ab initio calculations to generate reference data (structure and vibrational frequencies). Second, this approach has not been validated by comparing simulated guest adsorption or diffusion properties with experiment. Finally a fully bonded MOF model such as this will likely not display guestinduced changes in lattice parameters, as demonstrated in other fully bonded 46  and nonbonded 47  models.
An alternative approach is to perform simulations with framework atoms held at their crystallographic coordinates.
Short-range (van der Waals) potential parameters are usually taken from general force fields 63 64  and compatible parameters for guest atoms should successfully reproduce the bulk properties of the gas or liquid. Modeling of polyatomic guest molecules usually requires atomic charges for framework atoms, which are estimated from quantum cluster calculations of representative clusters. A promising advance in the estimation of framework atom charges came from Zhong and co-workers, 65 66  who are using a connectivity-based approach to assign an average charge based on an atom’s nearest neighbors. This approach saves both time and the computational cost of quantum calculations, further enabling a rapid computational screening of MOFs for materials properties. To date, they applied this technique to both $\mathrm{MOFs}^{[65]}$ and covalent organic frameworks (COFs). 66  For example excellent agreement between simulated adsorption isotherms using the new connectivity-based charges compared to quantum charges was achieved (Figure 5).
The vast majority of MOF modeling papers focus on gas adsorption properties using GCMC simulation. Fundamental issues associated with gas adsorption and separation by MOFs were recently reviewed 67  and will not be covered here. Of great interest for new materials development, however, is that the everincreasing use of general force field parameters for MOF atoms is allowing the use of molecular simulation as a screening tool, as summarized in Table 1  The majority of these studies involve energy-related gases such as methane, 50  carbon dioxide, 54–57 hydrogen, 58–60  or some combination of these, 48 51–53  but applications to more complex analytes also have been reported. 49 61 62 Although many modeling investigations focus on high-pressure conditions typical of industrial applications, comparison of adsorption energies at infinite dilution is relevant to chemical sensing, as seen in Figure 6 for a range of organic analytes and MOFs. These results exhibit a general increase in adsorption energy with analyte molar mass and the magnitude of the values suggests that the adsorption of light gases found in air will not interfere with chemical detection.
An excellent example of computational screening is shown in Figure 7  in which the cavity diameters of $504~\mathrm{MOF}$ structures are compared. The utility of these screening results is immediately evident by comparing the calculated pore limiting diameters with guest diameters, thereby narrowing the choice of MOFs with the optimum pore diameters for excluding a larger guest molecule in a mixture.
Another recent trend has been to use single-component adsorption isotherms from GCMC simulation as input parameters for continuum models, such as ideal adsorbed solution theory (IAST), 68  which predict adsorption isotherms for gas mixtures. Recent applications of GCMC and IAST to gas mixtures in a variety of MOFs and COFs are summarized in Table 2  Results from IAST calculations can also be combined with single-component diffusion coefficients obtained from MD simulations to predict gas permeability through MOF membranes. 69  Such calculations, which include membrane thickness and require single-component adsorption and diffusion data as input, can be used to predict mixture selectivity in MOF membranes (Figure 8).
The just described demonstrate the utility, and indeed, the essentialness of molecular modeling for MOF design. The enormous range of potential MOF structures, combined with the difficulty of synthesizing new structures, necessitates the use of computational approaches to design frameworks for specific applications. Moreover, the partial pressures typical of industrial and chemical sensing lie at opposite ends of the experimental spectrum, requiring costly and highly specialized equipment, in addition to the fact that many species of interest are toxic or otherwise dangerous to handle. It would appear then that the most effective approach to producing tailored MOFs involves an interdisciplinary team that includes both synthetic and modeling capabilities.
## 4. MOF-Based Separations
One of the most common uses of porous materials is in separation of mixtures of gases or liquids. With applications including purification of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and $\Nu_{2}$ removal of volatile organic compounds, and separation of $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ from $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ in natural gas, these materials play a vital role in many different industries. Separation relies on two mechanisms: thermodynamic, by which one component is selectively adsorbed over others in the mixture and kinetic, by which separation relies on the path the components take through the pores of the material. MOFs, with their potential for high surface area and synthetically tailorable porous structure, are well positioned to make a significant impact in this field. MOFs have been studied at length for applications in separation, and a recent extensive review catalogues the applications, design principles, and progress in this field. 67 Here we will list some of the most recent advances (Table 3) and discuss the use of MOFs in removing toxic and environmentally hazardous gases and the integration of MOFs into membranes, both relatively new areas of study.
## 4.1. MOFs for Removal of Toxic and Environmentally Hazardous Gases
Exploration of the use of MOFs in removing toxic or environmentally hazardous gases has been confined primarily to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ capture and separation from flue gas, 67 94  with some results concerning the removal of tetrahydrothiophene from fuel reported as well. 5  Most recently, Weireld and coworkers studied the uptake of hydrogen sulfide $\left(\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}\right)$ in MIL-47(V), MIL-53(Al, Cr, Fe), MIL-100(Cr) and MIL-101 $\mathrm{(Cr)}$ . $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{S}$ is a challenging adsorbate due to strong interactions with other porous materials, resulting in adsorbant decomposition or difficult regeneration. MIL-47(V) and MIL-53(Al, Cr) display little degradation and reversible uptake, however. The MIL-53 materials also exhibit step wise adsorption, which makes them attractive for pressure swing adsorption separations to purify natural gas. 87  Matzger and coworkers investigated the uptake of benzothiophene derivatives from isooctane solutions by MOF-177, MOF-5, UMCM-150, HKUST-1, and MOF-505, and found high levels of adsorption compared to zeolite Na(Y). Pore size played a key role in adsorption levels, with the best interactions found when the pore closely matched the size of the adsorbate. 88 This group continued this work to study the removal of dibenzothiophene and 4,6-dimethyldibenzothiophene from actual diesel fuel, obtaining high levels of adsorption, high selectivity, and feasible regeneration of adsorbent materials. 89  Yaghi and coworkers performed a large-scale study of the adsorption of various harmful gases, including sulfur dioxide, ammonia, chlorine, tetrahydrothiophene, benzene, dichloromethane, and ethylene oxide, by MOF-5, IRMOF-3, MOF-74, MOF-177,
HKUST-1, and IRMOF-62. These results were compared to BLP carbon, a common activated carbon. For each gas, at least one MOF exceeded the capacity of BLP carbon, and in the case of ammonia, the MOFs displayed 6–105 fold capacity enhancement. 90  High ammonia adsorption is also observed for composite MOF-5-graphite oxide (GO) materials. 95  These materials show strong dispersive forces at the interface between the MOF-5 and GO layers, which result in enhanced uptake of ammonia when compared to the parent materials. Researchers also found evidence that ammonia coordinates to the zinc clusters in the MOF-5 component, which not only increases adsorption capacity, but could also serve as a degradation pathway. 96 Similar synergistic effects on adsorption of hydrogen are observed in MOF-5/carbon nanotube composites. 97  These results indicate that MOF materials are promising adsorbents for harmful chemicals, although more research into their stability and large-scale production will be needed for MOFs to be integrated into industrial processes.
## 4.2. MOFs for Membrane-Based Separations
Membrane-based technologies are widely applied on an industrial scale to the separation of gases. 98  Membranes rely on a permeability differential between two gases, based on pore size and shape and chemical functionality. As these are tunable properties in MOFs, more so than in zeolites or other porous materials, they represent a promising new material class for separation membranes. Porous crystalline materials, such as MOFs, can obviate the tradeoff between selectivity and throughput that is often found in polymeric membranes. 99 MOFs can either be integrated into a polymer matrix or grown directly as thin films 6  Won et al. reported the first case of impregnating a polymer with a MOF, using a $\mathrm{Cu}\mathrm{-}4,4^{\prime}$ -bipyridine in amorphous glassy polysulfone, achieving very high selectivity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ mixtures, almost 200 with $5\%$ weight loading. 100 More recently, Perez et al. added MOF-5 nanocrystals to a Matrimid membrane. While the selectivities for various gas mixtures remained at similar values to the pristine polymer membrane, the permeability of the composite membrane was $120\%$ larger. 78
Liu et al. synthesized the first continuous MOF-5 membranes via standard heating solvothermal synthesis in the presence of $\alpha$ -alumina disks (Figure 9). Experiments to measure the permeation of ${\mathrm{SF}}_{6}$ $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ indicate that these gases exhibit Knudsen diffusion behavior and size-selective permeability. 93  Yoo and Jeong obtained similar results for MOF-5 continuous membranes grown via microwave rapid seeding followed by solvothermal secondary growth. 21  Guo et al. studied the first actual separation of gases employing a MOF membrane.
This group grew films of CuBTC on a 400-mesh copper net, then tested this composite for separation of $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ from binary mixtures with $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ $\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ and $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ The MOF membrane provided excellent permeation for $\mathrm{H}_{2}$ $(\sim1\times10^{-1}\ \mathrm{mol}\cdot\mathrm{m}^{-2}\cdot\mathrm{s}^{-1})$ and separation factors of 6–7, which exceed those expected from Knudson diffusion. The membrane maintains these separation factors for 25 hours and can be reused with little loss of function. 91  Another example is a $\mathrm{Cu}\mathrm{-}4,4^{\prime}$ -(hexafluoroisopropylidene)-bis(benzoic acid) MOF membrane that exhibits very high selectivity for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/$ $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ mixtures. Unfortunately, very low fluxes were observed, an effect attributed to pore blocking caused by the randomly oriented seed layer. 92  As discussed in a previous section, Caro and coworkers have grown ZIF-8 19  and ZIF-7 22  membranes using microwave-assisted solvothermal synthesis. Both materials exhibit size-based molecular sieving (Figure 10), with the ZIF-8 material having an $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ separation factor of 11.2, while the ZIF-7 membrane gives separation factors of 7.7 for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{N}_{2}$ and 5.9 for $\mathrm{H}_{2}/\mathrm{CH}_{4}$ .
These results demonstrate that MOF-based membranes are a promising technology, but one that is still very much in its infancy. As remarked upon above (Sect. 3.0), the simulation of transport through MOFs is progressing 69  and is beginning to provide valuable assistance in the selection of MOFs for particular separation processes. However, many of the difficulties associated with making robust, defect-free membranes have yet to be surmounted, and much more research will be needed if MOFs are to compete with zeolite 99  and other commercially available membranes.
## 5. MOF Guest Catalysts
One of the most prolific areas of MOF research is the field of catalysis. MOFs have several advantageous features in this regard. In particular, the inorganic-organic hybrid nature of their structure, coupled with their nanoporosity, provides multiple opportunities to create one or more catalytic sites within a pore. Most MOF catalysts rely on the framework metal, but clearly the organic linker can participate as well. Both the linker’s chemical functionality and shape (i.e. enantioselectivity) may be catalytically active. Alternatively, the linker may be used as a scaffold to which discrete catalytic complexes can be bound. This synthetic flexibility enables considerable control over pore size and pore environment, allowing selectivity to be tuned more effectively. MOF pores can also serve as hosts for guest molecules or as templates and supports for metal or metal oxide nanoclusters. Finally, all of these properties can be modified via chemical synthesis, which sets MOFs apart from other nanoporous materials such as zeolites and activated carbon. The topic of MOFs as catalysts was recently reviewed, 101,102  so we will present a list of the most recent MOF catalysts (Table 4  4a) and confine our discussion to that of guest-based catalysis, which has received comparatively less attention in previous reviews.
Fischer and coworkers carried out the early work in the field of MOF guest catalysis by depositing Pd and Cu nanoparticles in MOF-5 (IRMOF—1) via chemical vapor deposition (CVD). The Pd-MOF-5 composite catalyzed cyclooctene hydrogenation,
