while the Cu variant catalyzed methanol production from synthesis gas. 136  During the intervening years, several investigators explored MOF-5 as a host for nanoparticle catalysts. Sabo et al. infiltrated MOF-5 with ${\mathrm{Pd}}(\mathsf{a c a c})_{2}$ which was then converted to Pd nanoparticles with heating under vacuum. This material catalyzed the hydrogenation of styrene, 1-octene, and ciscyclooctene. The MOF material showed size selectivity, having much faster reaction with styrene than cyclooctene, and a higher rate of catalysis than Pd on activated carbon, although the material demonstrated low hydrothermal stability. 137  Fischer and coworkers also tested Ru-loaded MOF-5 as a catalyst for benzene hydrogenation and benzyl alcohol oxidation, but found low levels of conversion. 133  This same group also studied Cu and $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ loading in MOF-5 and found that the Cu-MOF-5 composite catalyzed methanol synthesis, a surprising result since pure copper shows almost no catalytic activity in the absence of $\mathrm{{znO}}$ . When $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ nanoparticles are also added, the MOF shows even higher activity, although this effect quickly degrades. 131 Finally, Ishida et al. deposited gold nanoclusters onto various MOFs, including MOF-5, MIL-53 (Al), CPL-1, CPL-2, and CuBTC, via solid grinding with $\mathrm{Me}_{3}\mathrm{Au}(\mathrm{acac})$ . These materials catalyze the oxidation of 1-phenylethanol oxidation, with the best yield $(79\%)$ obtained for MOF-5. 130
Other MOF frameworks in addition to MOF-5 have been used as templates for nanoparticles, including MOF-177. Proch et al. loaded MOF-177 with Pt nanoparticles via gas phase infiltration of a Pt complex followed by reduction. This material efficiently catalyzes the oxidation of benzylic and allylic alcohols, though the catalyst could not be recycled due to stability issues. 132 Jiang et al. deposited Au nanoparticles in ZIF-8 via grinding with a volatile organogold complex (Figure 11). These materials exhibit catalytic activity in the oxidation of CO, but only after activation at $300~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}.$ , and the materials showed reproducible activity on subsequent runs. 127  Park et al. load a mesoporous Tb- triazine-1,3,5-tribenzoate MOF with Ni nanoparticles. This material catalyzes the hydrogenation of styrene and the reduction of nitrobenzene to aniline with similar activity to Raney Ni. 129  In addition to noble metal nanoparticles, Keggin polyoxometalates (POMs) have been explored as catalytic MOF guests. Maksimchuk et al. reported the adsorption of Ti and Co-POMs into MIL-101(Cr). When tested as catalysts in the oxidation of $\alpha$ -pinene, caryophyllene, and cyclohexene with $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ or $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{2}$ the composite materials displayed comparable or better performance to the homogeneous POM catalysts. In particular, the Ti-POM/MIL-101 showed higher selectivity in the epoxidation of caryophyllene and greater allylic oxidation selectivity and percent conversion for the oxidation of cyclohexene. These catalysts proved to be recyclable, except in the case of the Ti-POM with cyclohexene. 134  Sun et al. grew crystals of CuBTC with encapsulated $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ POM. This material was used as an acid catalyst for ester hydrolysis and displayed higher activity per acid unit than other solid oxides, organic resins (i.e. Amberlyst-15), and liquid acids $\mathrm{(H}_{2}\mathrm{SO}_{4})$ . Size selectivity and good recyclability were also observed. 128  More recently, Kapteijn and coworkers synthesized MIL-101(Cr) MOFs with varying levels of encapsulated $\mathrm{H}_{3}\mathrm{PW}_{12}\mathrm{O}_{40}$ POMs. For comparison, they also added these POMs to the MOF via impregnation after synthesis. The encapsulated catalysts displayed very high activity and reusability in the Knoevenagel 138  condensation, esterification of of n-butanol, and in the dehydration of methanol to dimethyl ether, while the impregnated catalyst demonstrated lower activity, an effect attributed to the better dispersion of the POM in the encapsulated material. 135
## 6. Drug Delivery
With their tunable host-guest properties and facile modification via chemical synthesis, MOFs represent a very attractive platform for drug delivery systems. The early work in this field was carried out by Horcajada and coworkers in which they investigated the loading of ibuprofen into MIL-100(Cr) and MIL-101(Cr) and found that both have high uptake $(0.35~\mathrm{g/g}$ and $1.38~\mathrm{g/g}$ respectively) and 3–6 day release times in simulated body fluid. 168  This proof-of-concept work branched out to several other studies in the last few years. McKinlay et al. find that Co and Ni-2,5-dihydroxyterephthalate MOFs are able to bind NO at open coordination sites in levels higher than currently employed zeolites. These materials can store $6{\mathrm{-}}7\mathrm{mmol/g}$ of NO for several months, but on contact with PBS buffer they release the gas in about 10 minutes. Samples of loaded material placed in close proximity to porcine coronary arteries resulted in complete relaxation. 139  Covalent NO storage is reported using amine-functionalized IRMOF-3 and UMCM-1- $\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ Here, the NO reacts with the amine to form diazeniumdiolate groups. Decomposition of the framework in phosphate buffer results in release of NO gas, with values of $0.51\ \mathrm{mmol/g}$ for IRMOF-3 and $0.10\mathrm{mmol/g}$ for UMCM-1- $\cdot\mathrm{NH}_{3}$ Rosi and coworkers demonstrated storage and release of the antiarrythmia drug Procainamide from an anionic pillared zinc-adeninate MOF in PBS buffer. When the drug-loaded MOF was introduced into deionized water, drug release was significantly reduced, suggesting that cation exchange is crucial to this process. 140  MOFs have also been used as platforms to build multifunctional nanoparticles. Polymers functionalized with fluorescent contrast agents, targeting ligands, and therapeutic agents have been grafted onto a Gd-MOF nanoparticle core, which itself functions as an MRI contrast agent. 141
A large portion of the recent work in the field of MOF drug delivery is focused on MIL compounds. Lin and coworkers postsynthetically modified nanoparticles (NPs) of amine functionalized MIL-101(Fe) with BODIPY type dyes and cisplatin prodrugs. These MOFs decompose in water to release their covalently bound payloads and the rate of release can be modulated by coating the NPs with a thin layer of silica. These coated nanoparticles are able to cross the cell membranes of HT-29 tumor cells and either inhibit growth or stain the cells depending on their functionality. 142  Horcajada et al. revisited ibuprofen delivery with flexible MIL-53(Fe) and find that drug adsorption induces expansion in the MOF framework. Desorption times for this material were very slow (20 days) and had zero-order kinetics, effects attributed to the tight fit of the molecule in the framework. 143  This group very recently published a more comprehensive study of drug delivery with MIL nanoparticles in which they considered an array of five frameworks and nine drugs (Table 5). 144  For the anticancer drug busulfan, $32\%$ loading by weight was achieved in MIL-100, about 5 times higher than currently available drug delivery platforms. The busulfan-loaded MOF exhibited very similar cytotoxic activity to free busulfan. MIL-100 loaded with anti-HIV drug AZT-TP also showed significant in vitro inhibition of virus replication. Furthermore, in vitro cell studies and in vivo rat studies demonstrated the low toxicity of these Fe MOF nanoparticles. These results underline the promise of MOFs in this field and indicate that MOFs are ready for more extensive biological testing as drug delivery platforms.
## 7. Electronic and Optical Applications
With a diversity of topologies and linkers, MOFs represent a class of materials with unique optical and electronic properties. We recently reviewed the topic of luminescent MOFs, 145  compiling a list of over fifty emissive frameworks and describing the various pathways by which MOFs can emit light. Possible applications in this particular area of MOF research include electron and proton conducting materials, light emitting diode (LED) materials, nonlinear optical devices, and sensors, a topic which we will discuss in detail in a subsequent section.
## 7.1. Electronic Applications of MOFs
Obtaining electronic or ionic conductivity in MOFs would expand the range of potential applications to include electronic devices, electrical storage, and power generation. Proton conductivity in 1D and 2D coordination polymers has been explored previously, and this class of materials is of interest as components in proton exchange membrane and direct methanol fuel cells. Kitagawa and coworkers synthesized a $Z\mathrm{n}$ -oxalate-adipic acid MOF which contains $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ ions and water and carboxylate groups in its honeycomb-shaped pores. This material exhibits $8\times10^{-3}~\mathrm{S/cm}$ proton conduction at $25^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and $98\%$ relative humidity, a level of conductivity comparable to the polymer Nafion. The activation energy of this MOF is found to be 0.63 eV, substantially higher than other hydrated proton conductors, which suggests that its high conductivity is the result of conduction vehicles other than water, presumably the $\mathrm{NH_{4}^{+}}$ and free carboxylate groups. 146  Shimizu and coworkers reported a Na-2,4,6-trihydroxy-1,3,5-benzenetrisulfonate MOF with a honeycomb structure of 1D pores lined with sulfonate groups. This MOF exhibits proton conductivity of $5\times10^{-6}~\mathrm{S/cm}$ at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ when hydrated. Dehydration at temperatures higher than $70~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ reduces the conductivity of the MOF. If the MOF is instead loaded with 1H-1,2,4-triazole, its conductivity increases dramatically to $5\times10^{-4}$ at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under anhydrous conditions. As a proof-of-concept experiment, this loaded material was fabricated into a membrane and used in an $\mathrm{H}_{2}/$ air cell. Electromotive force measurements gave an open circuit voltage of $1.18{\mathrm{~V~}}$ at $100~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , 147  a promising result commending future MOF materials to this area of research.
## 7.2. Optical Applications of MOFs
In the last few years researchers began exploring the use of MOFs in light-emitting devices. Gandara et al. synthesized a series of (lanthanide)- $^{\cdot4,4^{\prime}}$ -(hexafluoroisopropylidene)bis(benzoic acid) MOFs, including lanthanides Y, La, Ce, Pr, Nd, Sm, Eu, Gd, Tb, Dy, Ho, Er, and Yb. The Eu and Tb variants are very efficient red and green emitters respectively, indicating possible use in LED applications. The Gd MOF emits broadly in the visible region, making it a candidate for white LEDs. 151  Wang et al. report an Ag-4-cyanobenzoate MOF with broad emission in the visible region. When irradiated at $330~\mathrm{nm}$ the MOF emits strongly at $566~\mathrm{nm}$ to give yellow light. However, when irradiated at $350\mathrm{nm}$ , the emission broadens considerably (Figure 13) to afford light that closely matches the Commission Internationale de l’Eclairage chromaticity coordinates for white light. 152
Infrared-emitting MOFs are also known. Rosi, Petoud and coworkers designed Yb-4,4 -[(2,5-Dimethoxy-1,4-phenylene)di-2,1- ethenediyl]bis-benzoate (PVDC) MOFs that have near-infrared (NIR) emission with excitation in the visible spectrum. 153 When these MOFs were synthesized with both Yb and Er, emission peaks are observed at $980\mathrm{nm}$ (Yb) and $1530\mathrm{nm}$ (Er). The intensity of these peaks varies linearly with the concentration of each metal in the MOF. Thus, these MOFs can act as NIR barcodes (Figure 14), with unique NIR spectra for varying levels of Yb and Er. 154  Nd  has also been integrated into these materials for a more sophisticated barcode signal, but controlling its level in the MOF has proved challenging. These materials retain their spectroscopic properties when immobilized in superglue, thus allowing for the fabrication of simple devices.
## 8. Sensing
MOF-based sensing has received considerable attention in the past few years, with detection of analytes ranging from water, alcohols, and metal cations, to radiation and nitro-explosives being demonstrated. With their highly ordered structures, and internal environments easily tunable by chemical synthesis, MOFs have a high potential for controllable analyte binding. Tunable pore size and functionality also offer the ability to control selectivity of analytes. MOFs offer numerous signal transduction pathways, and while researchers have exploited luminescence most extensively, new optical, mechanical, and electronic methods have surfaced recently, broadening the spectrum of applications for these materials.
## 8.1. Optical Sensing
The majority of MOF sensors reported employ the modulation of optical properties for their sensory response. Typically, this takes the form of increases or decreases in luminescence signal on exposure to the analyte. Most of the recent reports rely on
## www.MaterialsViews.com
metal-centered emission, as opposed to linker- or guest-centered luminescence. For example, coordination of ethanol to the metal center of a $\mathtt{E u-4},4^{\prime}$ -(hexafluoroisopropylidene)-bis(benzoic acid) MOF rapidly quenches its luminescence. A device built with this material and exposed to ethanol vapor shows a rapid signal decrease followed by almost complete signal regeneration under flowing air. 155  Similar quenching and reversibility are observed for a porous Zn—Ir(2-phenylpyridine) MOF, which displays $20\%$ signal reduction when exposed to 0.1 atm of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ Application of vacuum regenerates the MOF phosphorescence in 120 seconds. Similar nonporous MOFs are quenched by $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ but irreversibly. 156  Jiang et al. report a Cd-bipyridineaminoterephthalate MOF that has very weak luminescence in its desolvated state. Addition of various solvents increases the fluorescence signal, with water, THF, and methanol providing the strongest responses. 83  MOF-76 (Tb-benzenetricarboxylate) exhibits increased luminescence in MeOH when exposed to various anions, with fluoride providing the best response. The O-H bond stretching on the methanol quenches $\mathrm{Tb}^{3+}$ luminescence, but is itself suppressed by hydrogen bonding with fluoride. 157  Regeneration of the signal was achieved by soaking the MOF in pure MeOH for 24 hours. Cation-based quenching is observed for a Eu-pyridine-3,4-dicarboxylate MOF, which has free Lewis basic pyridyl sites. When metals bind to these sites, the efficiency of energy transfer from the organic linker to the $\mathrm{Eu}^{3+}$ metal centers is decreased and the fluorescence signal is diminished. $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ $\ensuremath{\mathrm{Mn}}^{2+}$ ${\mathrm{Co}}^{2+}$ , and $\mathrm{Cd}^{2+}$ all exhibit significant quenching, while no response is observed for alkali and alkaline-earth metal cations. 158  Eu-based MOF also are sensitive to $\mathrm{\pH}$ . In the range of 5–7.5, a Eu-1,10-phenanthroline-2,9-dicarboxylic acid MOF experiences a linear luminescence increase with increasing pH, a phenomenon that is completely reversible.
MOF luminescence and thus sensory response may also originate from guest molecules or the linker molecules themselves. Luo and Batten report an amine-functionalized Zn-1,2,4,5-benzenetetracarboxylate MOF that displays lanthanide luminescence when doped with $\mathrm{EuCl}_{3}$ or $\mathrm{Tb}(\mathrm{ClO}_{4})_{3}$ Exposure to aqueous metal ion solutions quenches the fluorescence via displacement of the lanthanide guest. Good selectivity for $\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}$ and ${\mathsf{C o}}^{2+}$ is observed for both dopants. 159  MOFs with linker-based emission have also been employed as sensors. Stylianou et al. synthesized an In-pyrenetetrabenzoic acid MOF with strong linker based emission. This MOF exhibits red-shifted emission from its as-synthesized compared to desolvated state. The MOF also exhibits solvatochromism, with different emission maxima and intensities when filled with different solvents. 160  Thin $Z\mathrm{n}$ -BTC films grown on a treated Zn surface display luminescence that is quenched by exposure dimethylamine. Signal response is strongest in the range of 8.57–25.71 ppm, and the MOF is selective for dimethylamine over larger amines such as aniline, n-butylamine and n-propylamine. The mechanism for quenching was studied by Raman and FTIR spectroscopy and is believed to be caused by disruption of carboxylate groups in the hydrogen bonded layers of the structure. 161  The system is stable for 10-cycles with little signal loss, but regeneration requires heating overnight under vacuum. Nitro compounds, such as 2,4-dinitrotoluene (DNT) and 2,3-dimethyl-2,3-dinitrobutane (DMNB) induce luminescence quenching in a pillared $Z\mathrm{n}{-}4,4^{\prime}$ -biphenyldicarboxylate −1,2-bipyridylethene MOF. 162  In thin films, the response times are very fast, with maximum quenching reached in about ten seconds (Figure 15). The sensitivity of these films for DMNB exceeds previously reported materials based on conjugated polymer thin films. Regeneration of this MOF is facile and can be accomplished by heating at $150^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for 1 minute, conditions that could be employed in a commercial device.
While most MOF sensors based on optical detection exploit fluorescence as their transduction mechanism, a few recent reports rely on other optical properties of MOFs. Lu and Hupp have grown ${\sim}1000\ \mathrm{nm}$ films of ZIF-8 on glass, a thickness that is similar to the wavelength of visible light and at which Fabry-Perot interference peaks may be visible. As these interference peaks depend on the refractive index of the film, the material can be employed as a sensor. Exposure to propane gas induces a partial pressure-sensitive red shift in the UV-vis transmission spectrum. This effect is size selective: propane and n-hexane can be detected this way, while the material is unresponsive to cyclohexane. The material also displays chemical selectivity: water produces no effect, while more hydrophobic ethanol can be detected at concentrations as low as $100\mathrm{ppm}$ . 163 MOFs can also be used to detect ionizing radiation. Doty et al. showed that both 2D and 3D $Z\mathrm{n}$ -stilbenedicarboxylate MOFs exhibit luminescence when exposed to high energy protons and alpha particles. 164  The 3D-MOF exhibits a large red shift in its ion beam-induced luminescence when compared to its fluorescence emission spectrum, indicating structural change on ionization (Figure 16). The luminosity of these materials is comparable to commercially available organic scintillators and they show good resistance to radiation damage, making this new class of scintillators attractive for a number of detection applications. Moreover, the crystalline structure of MOFs allows the interaction among the various linker-based chromophores in the structure to be precisely characterized and tuned, unlike liquid and polymer-based scintillators. As a result, there is potential to tune both the light output and timing in a rational way, representing an important advance in this field of material science.
## 8.2. Other Transduction Mechanisms
Three recent papers describe sensors that do not rely on MOF optical properties. Liu et al. have synthesized a Li-1,3-benzene dicarboxylate MOF with 1D pores and a hydrophilic pore environment. This material can detect small polar molecules such as water and methanol when coated on a quartz crystal microbalance, with detection limits near 1 ppm. More hydrophobic compounds, such as THF and acetone, show no response. The signal is regenerated by exposing the microbalance chamber to vacuum. 165  Achmann et al. incorporated various commercially available MOFs into impedance sensors and find that Fe-BTC is sensitive to water vapor and to a lesser extent ethanol and methanol, at $120^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . Regeneration is achieved quickly when the vapor is removed from the nitrogen stream. 166  Taking advantage of adsorbate-induced flexibility in CuBTC, Allendorf and coworkers deposited thin films of this MOF onto microcantilevers with built-in piezoresistive sensors for stress-based detection. Analytes such as water, methanol, and ethanol interact with the MOF framework and cause it to expand, inducing stress in the microcantilever that is detected as a resistance increase in the sensor. The signal returns to baseline when the analyte gas is replaced with a flow of dry nitrogen. Measurable response is observed within $500~\mathrm{{ms}}$ (Figure 17). Nitrogen,
## www.MaterialsViews.com
oxygen, and carbon dioxide do not afford a response, although if the physisorbed water in the HKUST pores is removed, the material becomes sensitive to $\mathrm{CO}_{2}$ due to the open coordination sites. 167  These examples illustrate the versatility of MOFs for sensing, which exceed the capabilities of many other material classes employed in such applications.
## 9. Conclusions
This review demonstrates that the properties of MOFs open the door to an incredible range of important applications, with potential implications for fields ranging from analytical chemistry to drug delivery. Nevertheless, these developments are still in their formative stages. MOF science is largely the domain of synthetic chemists at this time. While their work is producing an ever-increasing number of new MOFs, it is also important that scientists outside the field of synthetic chemistry apply their tools and expertise to build the fundamental and practical knowledge base required to bring these versatile materials from the laboratory into the realm of actual use. For example, aspects such as the fundamental origins of energy transfer within MOFs, mechanical properties, long-term stability, defects, and electrical and mechanical properties have received almost no focused attention. Practical large-scale synthetic methods must also be developed. It is therefore clear that, although more than fifteen years have passed since the first MOF was described, there are still many opportunities to conduct influential science and engineering with respect to this fast-growing category of unique materials.
